  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =y-4{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y-4{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=2.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=2.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{-1-4\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}}=-9 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-9\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+1=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2-\\frac{9}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-9$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0-4(-\\frac{5}{2})}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{10}{(\\frac{9}{4})}=\\frac{40}{9} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{40}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0-\\frac{5}{4}=-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{20}{9}=-\\frac{5}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-9$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{40}{9}$ | $\\frac{20}{9}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{18}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈-\\frac{5}{4}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈-\\frac{5}{18}.$",
